$DIR/main.p:11:13: p holds a view of v (v.Ptr(0), line 10), which was dropped by v.Drop() at line 12, but the call deferred at line 11 uses it when the function returns
    p looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    v.Ptr(0) at line 10. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used
    only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 12 v
    was dropped by v.Drop(), so the view may look at memory that was released,
    or at a value that now belongs to something else. The call deferred at line
    11 keeps what p held there and runs when the function returns, after the
    deferred calls made after it (section 12.19).
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 12, or drop v only after the last use of p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
